          "html": "<section class=\"revealPart\" id=\"focus-paragraph-4-0\">\n<h3>完整原文 · 第4段</h3>\n<p>Mies’s signature phrase means that less decoration, properly organized, has more impact than a lot. Elegance, he believed, did not derive from abundance. Like other modern architects, he employed metal, glass and laminated wood—materials that we take for granted today but that in the 1940s symbolized the future. Mies’s sophisticated presentation masked the fact that the spaces he designed were small and efficient, rather than big and often empty. The apartments in the elegant towers Mies built on Chicago’s Lake Shore Drive, for example, were smaller—two-bedroom units under 1,000 square feet—than those in their older neighbors along the city’s Gold Coast. But they were popular because of their airy glass walls, the views they afforded and the elegance of the buildings’ details and proportions, the architectural equivalent of the abstract art so popular at the time.</p>\n</section>\n<section class=\"revealPart\" id=\"focus-paragraph-4-1\">\n<h3>意群划分 · 第4段</h3>\n<p>Mies’s signature phrase means / that less decoration, / properly organized, / has more impact than a lot. / Elegance, / he believed, / did not derive from abundance. / Like other modern architects, / he employed metal, / glass and laminated wood—materials / that we take for granted today but / that in the 1940s symbolized the future. / Mies’s sophisticated presentation masked the fact / that the spaces he designed were small and efficient, / / rather than big and often empty. / The apartments in the elegant towers Mies built on Chicago’s Lake Shore Drive, / for example, / were smaller—two-bedroom units under 1,000 square feet—than those in their older neighbors along the city’s Gold Coast. / But they were popular / because of their airy glass walls, / the views they afforded and the elegance of the buildings’ details and proportions, / the architectural equivalent of the abstract art so popular at the time.</p>\n</section>\n<section class=\"revealPart\" id=\"focus-paragraph-4-2\">\n<h3>意群翻译 · 第4段</h3>\n<p style=\"line-height:1.8;color:#2563eb;font-weight:500\">密斯的标志性短语的意思是 / 更少的装饰 / 组织得当 / 影响比很多更大<br>优雅 / 他相信 / 并非源自丰富<br>和其他现代建筑师一样 / 他使用金属 / 玻璃和层压木材—材料 / 我们今天认为这是理所当然的，但是 / 20世纪40年代象征着未来<br>密斯复杂的演讲掩盖了事实 / 他设计的空间小而高效 / 而不是大而且常常是空的<br>位于芝加哥湖岸大道上的密斯优雅塔楼中的公寓 / 例如 / 比黄金海岸的老邻居更小，两居室单位面积不到 1,000 平方英尺<br>但他们很受欢迎 / 因为他们通风的玻璃墙 / 他们提供的景观以及建筑细节和比例的优雅 / 相当于当时流行的抽象艺术的建筑形式</p>\n</section>\n<section class=\"revealPart\" id=\"focus-paragraph-4-3\">\n<h3>标准译文 · 第4段</h3>\n<p>密斯的标志性短语意味着，较少的装饰，适当的组织，比大量的装饰具有更大的影响力。他相信，优雅并非源自丰富。与其他现代建筑师一样，他采用了金属、玻璃和层压木材——这些材料在今天我们看来是理所当然的，但在 20 世纪 40 年代却象征着未来。密斯复杂的演示掩盖了这样一个事实：他设计的空间小而高效，而不是大且经常空荡荡的。例如，密斯在芝加哥湖滨大道上建造的优雅塔楼中的公寓比位于城市黄金海岸的老邻居中的公寓要小，两居室单元面积不到 1,000 平方英尺。但它们之所以受欢迎，是因为它们通风的玻璃墙、提供的视野以及建筑细节和比例的优雅，相当于当时流行的抽象艺术的建筑风格。</p>\n</section>",
